artifact.self_contained_problem:
Develop a rigorous computational framework for simulating self-modifying open systems, i.e., dynamical computational systems in which not only the state but also the defining program, operators, structural relations, transition rules, and even the representational categories (the types, state spaces, or ontologies in which the system is described) are themselves time-varying objects subject to update by the system's own dynamics and by interaction with an environment.

Concretely, identify and characterize modeling paradigms and formal tools (for example: reflective and metacircular interpreters, higher-order rewriting systems, graph and hypergraph rewriting, membrane/P-systems, category-theoretic models such as functorial semantics with evolving signatures, open game/lens-based compositional semantics, agent-based and multi-level simulation frameworks, type systems supporting dynamic extension, or coalgebraic models of open systems) that can:

(i) represent a system S whose description ⟨P_t, Σ_t, R_t, C_t⟩ — program P_t, signature/operator set Σ_t, rule set R_t, and representational category C_t — is itself a function of time t;
(ii) admit a well-defined operational semantics in which transitions may rewrite components of ⟨P_t, Σ_t, R_t, C_t⟩, including changes of the ambient category C_t, while preserving a coherent notion of identity and history of S;
(iii) support openness, meaning interaction with an environment whose interface may also be renegotiated at runtime;
(iv) permit effective implementation, i.e., be realizable as an executable simulator on a standard computational substrate.

Provide such a paradigm together with theorems establishing its expressiveness, compositionality, and adequacy for modeling self-modifying open systems, or prove obstructions showing that no paradigm in a specified class can satisfy (i)–(iv) simultaneously.

